{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"2137e21b5d244b58","type":"group","x":310,"y":-40,"width":620,"height":3805,"label":"🖥️ Chain A — Exposed RDP (T1133)"},
		{"id":"4d0b0b5a1c094b36","type":"group","x":970,"y":-40,"width":620,"height":3805,"label":"📧 Chain B — Binary attachment (T1566 + T1036.007)"},
		{"id":"adff51f7fe66486a","type":"group","x":1630,"y":-40,"width":620,"height":3805,"label":"🔗 Chain C — LNK shortcut (T1566)"},
		{"id":"5483679733174a75","type":"group","x":2290,"y":-40,"width":620,"height":3805,"label":"💾 Chain D — Infected USB (T1091)"},
		{"id":"d1021873b5504ed2","type":"group","x":-40,"y":3845,"width":2970,"height":619,"label":"⚖️ Cross-chain comparison"},
		{"id":"b4fc39487a0f49b4","type":"text","text":"# 🧭 Attack Chains by Initial Access Technique\nThe Initial Access method **decides the whole chain**: who is driving, how Discovery looks, whether C2 is needed, and how the attacker gets back in (Persistence). Read each column top → bottom, then compare across to see where the chains diverge.\n\n**Why they differ**\n- 🖥️ **RDP** gives a *human in an interactive session* → GUI discovery under `explorer.exe`, often no C2 at first, and persistence through a **backdoor account** (they can simply log in again)\n- 📧 🔗 💾 **Attachment / LNK / USB** run *malware* with no door to walk back through → automated CMD/PowerShell discovery, a C2 implant, and **malware persistence** (service, scheduled task, Run key, Startup folder)\n\n🔴 attacker behavior · 🔵 detection · 🔶 pattern inferred from the source's logic, not stated verbatim · † ATT&CK ID added by me\nT1190 (exploited web app) is not charted: the source gives no chain or detections for it.","x":0,"y":-504,"width":2890,"height":344,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"be50d401a63a459f","type":"text","text":"## 👁️ At a glance\nWhat makes each chain unique","x":0,"y":0,"width":250,"height":347,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"90bcff15d01b42ff","type":"text","text":"## 1 · Initial Access\nTA0001","x":0,"y":447,"width":250,"height":458,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"3d0f893b27ed4955","type":"text","text":"## 2 · First actions\nDiscovery TA0007 · tool drop","x":0,"y":1005,"width":250,"height":562,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"38a63a5ad1724d99","type":"text","text":"## 3 · Tools & control\nIngress Tool Transfer T1105 · C2 TA0011","x":0,"y":1667,"width":250,"height":483,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"4072a4e4a91048fc","type":"text","text":"## 4 · Persistence\nTA0003","x":0,"y":2250,"width":250,"height":515,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"4774b567ef4841a7","type":"text","text":"### 🖥️ Exposed RDP\n**T1133** External Remote Services\n- **Driver:** a human in an interactive session\n- **First sign:** 4625 flood → 4624 (type 10)\n- **Process tree:** `explorer.exe` → admin GUI tools\n- **Signature move:** backdoor account, then RDP back in","x":330,"y":0,"width":580,"height":347,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"4c4687cd1c384d59","type":"text","text":"### 📧 Binary attachment\n**T1566** + **T1036.007**\n- **Driver:** the malware itself (automated)\n- **First sign:** archive in Downloads, then `invoice.pdf.exe`\n- **Process tree:** `explorer.exe` → malware → `cmd` / `powershell`\n- **Signature move:** discovery burst → C2 → malware persistence (or steal and exit)","x":990,"y":0,"width":580,"height":347,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"a9e885687fd54d53","type":"text","text":"### 🔗 LNK shortcut\n**T1566**\n- **Driver:** a script in the shortcut, then the downloaded RAT\n- **First sign:** `.lnk` created in Downloads; PowerShell with `explorer.exe` parent\n- **Process tree:** `explorer.exe` → `powershell.exe` → downloaded EXE\n- **Signature move:** a tiny stub fetches the real malware at once; the LNK launch itself is never logged","x":1650,"y":0,"width":580,"height":347,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"4e3a78bcb4b34656","type":"text","text":"### 💾 Infected USB\n**T1091** Removable Media\n- **Driver:** user double-clicks a disguised file; can spread with no further interaction\n- **First sign:** process `Image` on a non-C: drive (`E:\\`)\n- **Process tree:** `explorer.exe` → `E:\\malware.exe`\n- **Signature move:** bypasses the firewall; works with no Internet","x":2310,"y":0,"width":580,"height":347,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"cce3164963dc42cc","type":"text","text":"### 1️⃣ Scan → brute force → login\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- Botnet finds the exposed RDP port, brute-forces common usernames (Administrator, admin, support…)\n- ~100 attempts → password guessed (`Administrator:Summer2025`) → in\n- Or logs in directly with credentials it already knew — no brute force\n\n🔵 **Detect** — Security log\n1. **4625** + LogonType 3/10 + external IP → brute force\n2. **4624** → which **account** was used?\n3. 4624 type **10** → copy the **Logon ID**","x":330,"y":447,"width":580,"height":458,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"994c9df897874827","type":"text","text":"### 1️⃣ Archive → unpack → double-click\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- Phishing email with a `.zip` / `.rar` holding `invoice.pdf.exe` (extension hidden), or a `.com` / `.scr` / `.cpl`\n- The user opens it\n\n🔵 **Detect** — Sysmon\n1. **EID 1** browser started (parent `Explorer.EXE`)\n2. **EID 11** `invoice.zip` created in Downloads\n3. **EID 11** unpacked → `invoice.pdf.exe`\n4. **EID 1** executed, ParentImage `Explorer.EXE`","x":990,"y":447,"width":580,"height":458,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"3103e33b6f6f4cb2","type":"text","text":"### 1️⃣ Archive → shortcut → \"website\"\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- Phishing archive: a PDF plus `Visit Our Website!.lnk`\n- The LNK **Target** holds a PowerShell command; the user thinks it opens a site\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 11** — `.lnk` created in Downloads (look **backwards** from the alert)\n- LNK execution is **not logged**: PowerShell shows `explorer.exe` as parent\n- **EID 1** — the PowerShell command line\n- A LNK parser / EDR is needed to read the Target","x":1650,"y":447,"width":580,"height":458,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"017f438f039e4554","type":"text","text":"### 1️⃣ Plug in → run the decoy\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- Infected USB: a \"gift from HR\", or picked up from an infected print-shop PC\n- Real files hidden; decoys such as `RECOVERY.lnk`, `Photos.exe` with a folder icon, `photo_2024_1_12.jpg.exe`\n- The user launches it through the GUI\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 1** with `Image` on a **non-C: drive** (e.g. `E:\\malware.exe`)\n- `explorer.exe` parent — looks just like phishing","x":2310,"y":447,"width":580,"height":458,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"490f61d3f21d422b","type":"text","text":"### 2️⃣ Stub downloads the RAT\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- The PowerShell stub immediately downloads and starts the real malware:\n  `DownloadFile('.../r.exe','C:\\ProgramData\\r.exe'); start r.exe` (simplified RemcosRAT)\n- 🔶 Discovery then runs from the downloaded malware, as in Chain B\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 3** / DNS — `powershell.exe` connecting out\n- **EID 11** — `r.exe` written to `C:\\ProgramData`\n- **EID 1** — `r.exe` started, then its `cmd` / `powershell` discovery children","x":1650,"y":1005,"width":580,"height":562,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"49facc90669b4c18","type":"text","text":"### 2️⃣ Malware starts running\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- 🔶 Looks like the phishing chain: the malware starts, then runs CMD/PowerShell discovery as its children\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 1** tree: `explorer.exe` → `E:\\...` → `cmd.exe` / `powershell.exe`\n- A burst of discovery commands; confirm the **Image path is on the USB drive**","x":2310,"y":1005,"width":580,"height":562,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"c1bd307eae094471","type":"text","text":"## 5 · Collection & Exfiltration\nTA0009 · TA0006 · TA0010","x":0,"y":2865,"width":250,"height":425,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"4f6876a34da34e39","type":"text","text":"## 6 · Impact\nTA0040","x":0,"y":3390,"width":250,"height":335,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"0de39cf3b8584833","type":"text","text":"### 2️⃣ GUI discovery by a human\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- Same toolkit as an admin: Computer Management, Control Panel, Settings, Task Manager, Notepad (even Event Viewer)\n- CMD still possible out of habit\n- Example timing: logs back in ~2 hrs after the breach and reviews the Desktop\n\n🔵 **Detect** — Sysmon EID 1\n```\nexplorer.exe\n├── cmd.exe\n├── mmc.exe compmgmt.msc\n├── control.exe netconnections\n├── SystemSettings.exe\n├── notepad.exe ...\\secrets.txt\n└── taskmgr.exe\n```\nTie it to the 4624 type-10 **Logon ID**. Don't expect `whoami` spam.","x":330,"y":1005,"width":580,"height":562,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"85bdbd9f46cc41ec","type":"text","text":"### 2️⃣ Automated CMD / PowerShell burst\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- The malware runs discovery itself, right away: `whoami /priv`, `ipconfig`, `net user`, `dir`, `tasklist /v`, `wmic computersystem get model`, `Get-Service`, `Get-MpPreference`\n- May **delete itself** if a specific AV is present or the victim isn't a target\n\n🔵 **Detect** — Sysmon EID 1\n```\nexplorer.exe\n└── invoice.pdf.exe\n    ├── cmd.exe\n    │   └── ipconfig / whoami / net user ...\n    └── powershell.exe\n        └── Get-Service / Get-MpPreference\n```\nA **burst** of commands with the **malware as parent**.","x":990,"y":1005,"width":580,"height":562,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"5a6c7627d1a341d8","type":"text","text":"### 3️⃣ Tools by copy-paste; C2 optional\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- Tools arrive by **copy-paste over RDP** or a browser download — no CMD needed — or via `certutil` / `curl` / `Invoke-WebRequest`\n- C2 often **not needed**: the actor types in the RDP session. But RDP can be closed, so most still set up C2 quickly\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 3** / DNS from the downloading process; **EID 11** dropped file\n- **EID 1** command lines (`certutil -urlcache`, `curl.exe`, IWR)\n- Later C2 = a new process with outbound **EID 3**","x":330,"y":1667,"width":580,"height":483,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ed24c78b86654ccc","type":"text","text":"### 3️⃣ Implant connects back\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- **Simplest C2:** the attachment itself connects back and waits 24/7 (e.g. Cobalt Strike)\n- **Advanced:** downloads a second-stage C2 to `C:\\Temp` and runs it as a new process — survives deletion of the attachment\n- Pulls more tools only when needed (Mimikatz, Seatbelt, a RAT, finally ransomware)\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 3** — malware making outbound connections (network logs may show C2 **before** endpoint logs show the process)\n- Chain: **EID 11** drop in `C:\\Temp` → **EID 1** new process → **EID 3** connection","x":990,"y":1667,"width":580,"height":483,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"af7c05522c2d4384","type":"text","text":"### 4️⃣ Backdoor account — log back in\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- Create a user (**T1136**): `net user \"mr.backd00r\" \"p@ssw0rd!\" /add` or `lusrmgr.msc`\n- Add it to **Administrators** / **Remote Desktop Users** (**T1098.007**) — a new user alone can't RDP or admin\n- Or reset the password of an old, unused account\n- Then simply RDP back in. Optional extras: web shell (T1505.003), a service or task\n\n🔵 **Detect** — Security log\n- **4720** created · **4732** added to group · **4724** password reset · 4722 / 4738\n- Ask **who** created it, from **which IP / time**, and **what else** happened in that session (Subject Logon ID ↔ 4624)","x":330,"y":2250,"width":580,"height":515,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ffc29c60db4845f8","type":"text","text":"### 4️⃣ Malware must survive reboot\n🔴 **Attacker** — no RDP door to reuse\n- **Service** (admin): `sc create ... start= auto` · T1543.003\n- **Scheduled task** (most common): `schtasks /create ... /sc onstart` · T1053.005†\n- **Run key / Startup folder** (that user's logins): T1547.001\n- Data stealers skip this and exit within minutes\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- Service: **EID 1** `sc.exe` · **4697** / **7045** · child of [[services.exe]]\n- Task: **EID 1** `schtasks.exe` · **4698** · child of `svchost.exe -s Schedule` ([[svchost.exe]])\n- Startup folder: **EID 11** · Run key: **EID 13**","x":990,"y":2250,"width":580,"height":515,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"3880201a46f14d76","type":"text","text":"### 5️⃣ Manual hunting by the operator\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- Opens files of interest in Notepad / Wordpad, searches with `findstr`, copies to `C:\\Temp`, archives with 7-Zip or `Compress-Archive` (the BlackSuit pattern)\n- Uploads to cloud storage, GitHub, Telegram or a lookalike domain · T1567.002\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 1**: many `notepad` / `wordpad` processes on financial docs; `findstr`, `Get-ChildItem -Recurse`, `7za.exe a -tzip`\n- **EID 3** / DNS: the upload connection","x":330,"y":2865,"width":580,"height":425,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"20e0c2f54e134395","type":"text","text":"### 5️⃣ Script or stealer — fast and quiet\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- **Stealer / script:** collects and exfiltrates in under a minute (e.g. Gremlin: VPN profiles, wallets, browser sessions, chat apps), then exits — **no persistence**\n- **Persisted malware:** an operator can collect later by hand\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- Scripts: **EID 1** (`copy ...\\Signal`, `Compress-Archive`, `type | findstr`)\n- Stealers use their **own code** — no CMD trail — so rely on **EID 3** and the chain above","x":990,"y":2865,"width":580,"height":425,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"7163a4b4ca39465b","type":"text","text":"### 3️⃣ The RAT is the C2\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- The downloaded **RemcosRAT** is the implant: it connects back and gives full control\n- More tools later via `certutil` / `curl` / `Invoke-WebRequest` if needed\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 3** — `r.exe` (or whatever the stub dropped) connecting out\n- Same chain: **EID 11** → **EID 1** → **EID 3**\n- Judge the **process + destination + downloaded file** together","x":1650,"y":1667,"width":580,"height":483,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"c50dcd3312144457","type":"text","text":"### 3️⃣ C2 may not exist\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- The source notes USB infections can start **without Internet** and keep spreading without user interaction\n- 🔶 With Internet access, C2 works as in Chain B\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 3** if the host is online\n- 🔶 Look for the same malware on other drives and hosts — it spreads on its own","x":2310,"y":1667,"width":580,"height":483,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"39202052b3bb4bed","type":"text","text":"### 4️⃣ Same malware-persistence menu\n🔴 **Attacker** — no RDP door here either\n- Service · scheduled task · Run key · Startup folder (see Chain B)\n- The RAT needs to keep its C2 alive after a reboot\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- Service: **EID 1** `sc.exe` · **4697** / **7045** · parent [[services.exe]]\n- Task: **EID 1** `schtasks.exe` · **4698** · parent `svchost.exe -s Schedule`\n- Startup folder: **EID 11** · Run key: **EID 13** (`Image` = who wrote it)","x":1650,"y":2250,"width":580,"height":515,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"267728e951e34911","type":"text","text":"### 4️⃣ Same malware-persistence menu\n🔴 **Attacker** 🔶\n- Service · scheduled task · Run key · Startup folder — as in Chain B\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- Service: **EID 1** `sc.exe` · **4697** / **7045**\n- Task: **EID 1** `schtasks.exe` · **4698**\n- Startup folder: **EID 11** · Run key: **EID 13**\n- Per-user methods run through `explorer.exe` at login, so they blend in — anchor on the **file / registry write**","x":2310,"y":2250,"width":580,"height":515,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"2cebfdef3a9443ad","type":"text","text":"### 5️⃣ RAT-driven collection\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- With a RAT in place, an operator (or a built-in stealer function) collects: files, browser data, wallets, `.ssh`, databases\n- Archive in `C:\\Temp`, then upload (cloud storage, GitHub, Telegram, lookalike domain)\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 1** collection / archive commands launched by the RAT or its children\n- **EID 3** / DNS to the upload destination\n- Staged archive in **EID 11**","x":1650,"y":2865,"width":580,"height":425,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"8c5fb46eb85c4b41","type":"text","text":"### 5️⃣ Same options, network permitting\n🔴 **Attacker** 🔶\n- Same collection options as Chain B; exfiltration needs an Internet path\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n- **EID 1** collection commands from the USB-origin process tree\n- **EID 3** if exfiltration is attempted","x":2310,"y":2865,"width":580,"height":425,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"8a3ffbf7439e49f0","type":"text","text":"### 6️⃣ RDP → ransomware\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- RDP is nicknamed the **Ransomware Deployment Protocol**: an RDP breach often ends directly in ransomware\n- In an AD network one host can halt the whole company (McLaren hospitals: 743,000 patients)\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n⚠️ Not covered in your source notes.","x":330,"y":3390,"width":580,"height":335,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"c98216ba7fe04948","type":"text","text":"### 6️⃣ Stealer ends, implant escalates\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- Stealer: the chain **ends at exfiltration**\n- Implant: the foothold becomes a network entry point; ransomware is the final tool downloaded (IcedID → Dagon Locker took 29 days)\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n⚠️ Not covered in your source notes.","x":990,"y":3390,"width":580,"height":335,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"df0291f693c642eb","type":"text","text":"### 6️⃣ RAT foothold → ransomware\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- The RAT foothold can lead to the full chain: discovery, credentials, ransomware as the last downloaded tool\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n⚠️ Not covered in your source notes.","x":1650,"y":3390,"width":580,"height":335,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"03f91db9e05d46ab","type":"text","text":"### 6️⃣ Worm spread / ransomware delivery\n🔴 **Attacker**\n- Self-spreading infections (Camaro Dragon, Raspberry Robin)\n- The \"gift\" USB case your source links was a real **ransomware** delivery\n\n🔵 **Detect**\n⚠️ Not covered in your source notes.","x":2310,"y":3390,"width":580,"height":335,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"5f9fbef3d98440e3","type":"text","text":"## ⚖️ Side-by-side — how the chains differ\n| | 🖥️ A · RDP | 📧 B · Binary | 🔗 C · LNK | 💾 D · USB |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Who drives** | Human, interactive | Malware, automated | Script, then RAT | User launches, malware runs |\n| **Parent of first commands** | `explorer.exe` (GUI tools) | The malware EXE | `powershell.exe` from `explorer.exe` | The `E:\\` EXE |\n| **Discovery** | GUI | CMD / PowerShell burst | From the downloaded RAT 🔶 | Like phishing 🔶 |\n| **C2** | Optional at first | Needed: implant | The RAT | Maybe none |\n| **Persistence** | Backdoor account (4720 / 4732) | Service / task / Run key / Startup | Same menu | Same menu 🔶 |\n| **Best pivot** | **Logon ID** from 4624 type 10 | EID 11 → EID 1 file chain in Downloads | `.lnk` EID 11 + PowerShell with `explorer.exe` parent | **Drive letter** in `Image` |\n| **Core log** | Security 4624 / 4625 / 4720 / 4732 | Sysmon 1 / 11 | Sysmon 1 / 11 + LNK parser | Sysmon 1 |","x":330,"y":3885,"width":1700,"height":539,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"09f2773fd0094529","type":"text","text":"## 🔎 How to tell which chain you're in\n1. **4624 type 10** from an external IP, then admin GUI tools (`mmc.exe`, `taskmgr.exe`, `control.exe`) under `explorer.exe` with the same **Logon ID** → **Chain A · RDP**\n2. **Archive in Downloads → unpacked EXE → EXE executed** (EID 11, 11, 1) → **Chain B · Binary**\n3. **`.lnk` created, then `powershell.exe` with an `explorer.exe` parent**, then a downloaded EXE in `ProgramData` / `Temp` → **Chain C · LNK**\n4. **Process `Image` on a non-C: drive** (`E:\\...`) → **Chain D · USB**\n\nB, C and D all show `explorer.exe` as the parent of the first process — the **file-creation history** and the **drive letter** are what separate them. See [[explorer.exe]].","x":2110,"y":3885,"width":780,"height":539,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"743615409cc84df1","type":"text","text":"## ⚖️ Compare\nSpot the differences, then use the recognition guide on live alerts","x":0,"y":3885,"width":250,"height":539,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"0c82a9c7d6ad0216","x":-680,"y":1080,"width":570,"height":240,"type":"text","text":"attacker can use explorer.exe as a LOLBins by \nIn order to create a child process of explorer.exe parent, we can execute the following command:\n```\nC:\\Users\\thm> explorer.exe/root,\"C:\\Windows\\System32\\calc.exe\"\n```\n \n ==so `explorer.exe`  does NOT guarantee that he got here by RDP=="}
	],
	"edges":[
		{"id":"df7837cd151d4fcf","fromNode":"cce3164963dc42cc","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"0de39cf3b8584833","toSide":"top","label":"same RDP session (Logon ID)"},
